Hibernate + Spring Data JPA — Complete Interview Notes (1–2 YOE)
================================================================

1. What is JPA Difference between JPA and Hibernate
--------------------------------------------------

JPA (Java Persistence API) is a specification that defines how Java objects  
should be mapped to database tables. It provides interfaces like EntityManager  
but does not provide implementation.

Hibernate is a framework and implementation of JPA that performs ORM operations  
like mapping objects to tables, executing SQL, and caching.

Key Point

JPA is a specification  
Hibernate is an implementation  

____________________________________________________________________________________

2. What is ORM
--------------

ORM (Object Relational Mapping) is a technique used to map Java objects to  
database tables so developers can work with objects instead of writing SQL  
queries manually.

It reduces boilerplate SQL code and complexity of database handling.

____________________________________________________________________________________

3. What is an Entity
-------------------

An Entity is a Java class mapped to a database table using @Entity.  
Each object represents a row in the table.

Rules

Must have a primary key using @Id  
Should have a no argument constructor  
Must be a POJO class  

____________________________________________________________________________________

4. What is LAZY vs EAGER Loading
-------------------------------

LAZY loading means data is fetched only when required.  
EAGER loading means data is fetched immediately along with the parent entity.

Example

Employee and Tasks  
LAZY means tasks are loaded only when accessed  
EAGER means tasks are loaded immediately  

Best Practice

Prefer LAZY loading for better performance  

____________________________________________________________________________________

5. What is LazyInitializationException
-------------------------------------

LazyInitializationException occurs when we try to access a LAZY loaded entity  
outside of an active session or transaction.

Example

Fetch employee  
Close transaction  
Access employee.getTasks leads to exception  

Solutions

Use JOIN FETCH  
Use DTO projection  
Access data inside transaction  

____________________________________________________________________________________

6. What is JPQL vs Native Query
------------------------------

JPQL works with entity objects and is database independent.  
Native Query works directly with database tables using SQL.

Example

JPQL SELECT e FROM Employee e  
SQL SELECT * FROM employee  

Key Point

JPQL is database independent  
SQL is database specific  

____________________________________________________________________________________

7. What is Spring Data JPA
-------------------------

Spring Data JPA is a framework that simplifies database operations by providing  
built in repository interfaces and reducing boilerplate code.

Features

CRUD operations  
Pagination and sorting  
Method name based queries  
Custom queries using @Query  

____________________________________________________________________________________

8. What is N plus 1 Problem
--------------------------

N plus 1 problem occurs when one query fetches parent data and additional  
queries are executed for each record to fetch related data.

Example

Fetch employees uses one query  
Fetch tasks for each employee uses multiple queries  

Total queries equals 1 plus N  

Impact

Performance issues  
Increased database load  

Solution

Use JOIN FETCH  
Use EntityGraph  

____________________________________________________________________________________

9. What is Optimistic Locking
----------------------------

Optimistic locking is a mechanism used to handle concurrent updates without  
locking the database rows. It assumes that conflicts are rare.

Implementation

Uses @Version annotation  

Working

Each record has a version number  
When updating, Hibernate checks if the version is the same  
If version is changed by another transaction, update fails  

Advantage

Better performance as no database locking is used  

____________________________________________________________________________________

10. What is Pessimistic Locking
------------------------------

Pessimistic locking is a mechanism where the database row is locked when it is  
read, preventing other transactions from modifying it.

Working

When one transaction reads data, it locks the row  
Other transactions must wait until the lock is released  

Types

PESSIMISTIC READ  
PESSIMISTIC WRITE  

Advantage

Prevents data conflicts completely  

Disadvantage

Can reduce performance due to locking  

____________________________________________________________________________________

11. What are ACID Properties
---------------------------

ACID properties ensure that database transactions are reliable and consistent.  
They stand for Atomicity, Consistency, Isolation, and Durability.

____________________________________________________________________________________

12. Atomicity
------------

Atomicity means a transaction is executed completely or not at all.  
If any step fails, the entire transaction is rolled back.

____________________________________________________________________________________

13. Consistency
--------------

Consistency ensures the database moves from one valid state to another  
while following all rules and constraints.

____________________________________________________________________________________

14. Isolation
------------

Isolation ensures multiple transactions execute independently  
without interfering with each other’s data.

____________________________________________________________________________________

15. Durability
-------------

Durability ensures once a transaction is committed, data is permanently stored  
and will not be lost even in case of system failure.

____________________________________________________________________________________

16. What are Entity Lifecycle States
-----------------------------------

There are four states in JPA

Transient means object is not connected to database  
Persistent means object is managed by persistence context  
Detached means object exists but not managed  
Removed means object is marked for deletion  

____________________________________________________________________________________

17. What is Persistence Context
------------------------------

Persistence Context is a first level cache managed by EntityManager  
It stores all managed entities within a transaction  

It ensures only one instance per entity id  
and reduces database calls  

____________________________________________________________________________________

18. Difference between EntityManager and Session
-----------------------------------------------

EntityManager is a JPA interface  
Session is Hibernate specific implementation  

EntityManager is portable  
Session provides additional features  

____________________________________________________________________________________

19. What is Dirty Checking
-------------------------

Dirty checking is a mechanism where Hibernate automatically detects  
changes in a managed entity and updates the database  

It happens during flush or commit  
No need to call save after update  

____________________________________________________________________________________

20. What is Flush vs Commit
--------------------------

Flush synchronizes changes with database but does not permanently save  
Commit permanently saves changes and ends transaction  

Flush can happen multiple times  
Commit happens once  

____________________________________________________________________________________

21. What is mappedBy
-------------------

mappedBy defines the inverse side of relationship  
Owning side controls the database mapping  

____________________________________________________________________________________

23. What is Transactional
------------------------

@Transactional is used to manage transactions  

If exception occurs transaction is rolled back automatically  

____________________________________________________________________________________

24. What is Cascade
------------------

Cascade defines how operations like persist merge delete  
are applied from parent to child entities  

____________________________________________________________________________________

25. What is Orphan Removal
-------------------------

Orphan removal deletes child entity when it is removed  
from parent collection  

____________________________________________________________________________________

26. What happens if @Transactional is applied at class level vs method level
---------------------------------------------------------------------------

When @Transactional is applied at class level, all public methods of that class  
become transactional by default.

When @Transactional is applied at method level, it applies only to that  
specific method and gives more control.

If both class and method level are used, method-level annotation overrides  
the class-level configuration.

____________________________________________________________________________________

27. What happens if we call transactional method inside same class
----------------------------------------------------------------

When a method inside the same class calls another transactional method,  
Spring does not apply transaction on the second method.

This happens because Spring uses proxy-based AOP and internal method calls  
bypass the proxy.

So the second method runs without its own transactional behaviour.

____________________________________________________________________________________

28. What happens if a transactional method calls another transactional method
---------------------------------------------------------------------------

By default, both methods run in the same transaction because propagation  
type is REQUIRED.

If propagation is changed to REQUIRES_NEW, the second method will run in a  
separate transaction.

